{"clock":1,"nonce":"52db4dc9e50ad63e59b700ed10700fce","ops":[{"kind":"link","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","forge_id":"5954142201","number":"29","url":"https://github.com/guilbep/git-board/issues/29"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"title","value":"github: add EPFL-ENAC's issue types and settle its Priority field"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"body","value":"The fork's `native-fields` build turns kinds into GitHub issue types where EPFL-ENAC has one, and keeps them as labels otherwise. This is what the build needs from you, as an org owner or with one. The decision doc is `doc/design/bridges/native-fields.md` on the fork's local `native-fields` branch, which isn't pushed.\n\n## 1. Issue types in EPFL-ENAC\n\nSettings › Planning › Issue types › Create new type. All six are optional: until a type exists, its kind stays a label on GitHub, and nothing breaks. Each type shows in the type picker of every EPFL-ENAC repository (31 use types today), so it's the organization's call. With all six, the org has 9 of the 25 types allowed.\n\n- [ ] **Polish**: A small improvement to something that works\n- [ ] **Performance**: Faster or lighter, same behaviour\n- [ ] **Refactor**: A code change with no change in behaviour\n- [ ] **Documentation**: Docs only\n- [ ] **Style**: Formatting only\n- [ ] **Test**: Tests only\n\nAlready there: **Bug**, **Feature** and **Task**. Task has no git-board kind, so it comes in as `kind:task`.\n\nco2's kinds are flat labels, so once the types exist its bridges need:\n\n```\nkind-types = bug=Bug,feat=Feature,polish=Polish,perf=Performance,refactor=Refactor,docs=Documentation,style=Style,test=Test\n```\n\ngit-board can't write that key yet: its config doesn't hold each kind's type name (feat is Feature). That's a git-board follow-up. Until then, set the key by hand in each clone that syncs: `git config git-bug.bridge.\u003cname\u003e.kind-types …`.\n\nOn a flat project like co2, a kind must be in the key **before** its type is used. Otherwise an issue typed Polish comes in as `kind:polish` beside `polish`.\n\n## 2. The Priority issue field\n\nEPFL-ENAC already has organization issue fields: **Priority** (Urgent, High, Medium, Low), **Effort** (High, Medium, Low), **Start date** and **Target date**.\n\n- [ ] Check that Priority's four options fit `prio:p0`–`p3` (p0 = Urgent, p1 = High, p2 = Medium, p3 = Low). Complete it if you want other names or descriptions.\n- [ ] Decide whether `prio:` should become that field natively, like kinds. It would be a later build, not part of `native-fields`.\n\n## 3. A scratch repository for the live test\n\n- [ ] A **private** repository in EPFL-ENAC, for example `EPFL-ENAC/git-bug-native-fields-test`\n- [ ] guilbep has write access to it\n- [ ] The token the bridge uses can reach it. A fine-grained token needs the repository, Issues read and write, Metadata read, and an admin's approval if the org asks for one; a classic token needs `repo`.\n- [ ] It stays out of Project 40 and its automation\n\n## Facts behind it (checked 2026-10-21)\n\n- A user-owned repository has no issue types: `guilbep/git-bug-sync-test` answers `issueTypes: null`. So the live test needs an organization repository.\n- EPFL-ENAC has 542 typed issues in 31 repositories; 65 are in co2-calculator (28 Bug, 33 Feature, 4 Task).\n- gitlab.epfl.ch (19.4.2, free edition) has milestones but no iterations. Sprints become existing milestones there; nothing to add.\n\nDone when the types you want exist, the Priority decision is written here, and the scratch repository exists. Then the `native-fields` session runs the live test.\n"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"status","value":"open"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"labels","value":"status:backlog"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"assignees","value":"161889"}},{"kind":"seen","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793004401,"data":{"issue":"c7a7326978c78072ea88539ed084c9d7388b33ee","field":"comment:6313343349","value":"Update, 2026-10-21. native-fields is merged locally into the fork's `bridge-sync`, at 0420c69f, not pushed yet.\n\n- **Priority:** ADR 0001 settles it. Priority stays on the Project's Priority field (P0 to P3), because an org issue field can't be a Project column. Mapping `prio:` to the org's Priority field isn't worth a build: you can skip that checkbox.\n- **The six types:** still optional. Without them, those kinds stay labels on GitHub. Note that ADR 0001's own convention uses Bug, Feature and Task only.\n- **The scratch repository:** still needed. The live test it unblocks is #31.\n"}}]}